1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:07,500
A graph is a set of vertices and a set of edges. An algorithm is handed one of two representations, and the choice changes its running time.

2
00:00:07,500 --> 00:00:15,000
The first question is reachability: starting from a source, which vertices can I get to, and how far away are they in edges?

3
00:00:15,000 --> 00:00:22,500
The second question is connection: can I join every vertex into one tree of minimum total weight? That is the minimum spanning tree.

4
00:00:22,500 --> 00:00:30,000
The third question is travel: from one source, what is the cheapest route to every other vertex? Three families answer it, and which one is legal depends on the weights.

5
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:37,500
Breadth-first search from A. A holds a queue, and the source goes in first with distance zero.

6
00:00:37,500 --> 00:00:45,000
Dequeue A, and give every unvisited neighbour the distance one and a place in the queue. That is level one: B and C.

7
00:00:45,000 --> 00:00:52,500
Level two is D, E and F; level three is G; level four is H. The tree records one shortest route to each vertex.

8
00:00:52,500 --> 00:01:00,000
Every edge that is not in the BFS tree joins two vertices whose levels differ by at most one. That is the property GATE asked about in 2015.

9
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:07,500
Depth-first search from A. Instead of a queue it uses a stack, which is what a recursive call amounts to.

10
00:01:07,500 --> 00:01:15,000
Discovery times are written on the way in: A gets one, B two, D three, and so on down the chain to H at eight.

11
00:01:15,000 --> 00:01:22,500
Finish times are written on the way out, so every vertex finishes after all of its descendants. That nesting is the parenthesis property.

12
00:01:22,500 --> 00:01:30,000
Three edges are not in the DFS tree: A-C, D-E and C-F. Each joins a vertex to its own ancestor, so each is a back edge.

13
00:01:30,000 --> 00:01:37,500
Both traversals visit every vertex and every edge once, so both cost the number of vertices plus the number of edges on a list.

14
00:01:37,500 --> 00:01:45,000
BFS gives the fewest-edges distance. DFS gives no distance at all, and on this graph its tree is seven levels deep where BFS needs four.

15
00:01:45,000 --> 00:01:52,500
So each traversal is the tool for a different job: BFS for distance and levels, DFS for structure, cycles, and order.

16
00:01:52,500 --> 00:02:00,000
Neither traversal looks at weights, so neither can answer a question about cost. Weighted questions need the two algorithms of the next scenes.

17
00:02:00,000 --> 00:02:07,500
A cut splits the vertices into two groups. An edge crosses the cut when its two ends are in different groups.

18
00:02:07,500 --> 00:02:15,000
The cut property: the lightest edge crossing a cut belongs to some minimum spanning tree. If its weight is unique, it belongs to every one.

19
00:02:15,000 --> 00:02:22,500
The cycle property: the heaviest edge on a cycle belongs to no minimum spanning tree, provided it is strictly heavier than the rest.

20
00:02:22,500 --> 00:02:30,000
Distinct weights force a unique minimum tree, but a unique minimum tree does not need distinct weights. The implication runs one way only.

21
00:02:30,000 --> 00:02:37,500
Kruskal sorts all nine edges by weight and walks the list once, from the lightest to the heaviest.

22
00:02:37,500 --> 00:02:45,000
An edge is accepted unless its two endpoints are already connected. Union-find answers that question in almost constant time per edge.

23
00:02:45,000 --> 00:02:52,500
Five edges are accepted and four are rejected. The accepted set is B-C, A-B, B-D, D-F and E-F, with total weight 24.

24
00:02:52,500 --> 00:03:00,000
Why it is correct: every accepted edge is the lightest edge across the cut formed by its two components at that moment, so the cut property applies.

25
00:03:00,000 --> 00:03:07,500
Prim also starts at A, but it grows one tree instead of a forest. Every vertex outside the tree keeps a key: the cheapest edge joining it to the tree.

26
00:03:07,500 --> 00:03:15,000
Extract the smallest key, add that vertex, then lower the keys of its neighbours. From A, B and C both offer four, so the tie is broken by the rule this page states.

27
00:03:15,000 --> 00:03:22,500
The extraction order is A, C, B, D, F, E and the total is 24, the same number Kruskal produced by a different route.

28
00:03:22,500 --> 00:03:30,000
The cost depends on the data structure: a binary heap gives E log V, a plain array scanning for the minimum gives V squared.

29
00:03:30,000 --> 00:03:37,500
The 2024 question gives a seven-vertex graph and asks how many distinct minimum spanning trees it has.

30
00:03:37,500 --> 00:03:45,000
All four weight-one edges are forced. No cheaper path joins their endpoints, so by the cut property every minimum tree contains all four.

31
00:03:45,000 --> 00:03:52,500
Those four edges collapse the graph into three pieces: the pair A, B, F; the triple C, D, E; and the centre vertex G on its own.

32
00:03:52,500 --> 00:04:00,000
One edge must join G to the first piece and one to the second, chosen independently: three ways each, so nine trees, total weight eight.

33
00:04:00,000 --> 00:04:07,500
Dijkstra on a directed graph with non-negative weights. Every distance starts at infinity except the source, which is zero.

34
00:04:07,500 --> 00:04:15,000
Settle s, then relax: A becomes four and B becomes one. B is the smallest unsettled, so B is settled next.

35
00:04:15,000 --> 00:04:22,500
B settles at one and improves A to three. Then A at three, D at seven, C at eight, and finally T at eight.

36
00:04:22,500 --> 00:04:30,000
The guarantee rests on one fact: once a vertex is settled, no later route can be shorter, because every edge is non-negative.

37
00:04:30,000 --> 00:04:37,500
Four vertices, one negative edge: s to A costs one, s to B costs two, and B to A costs minus two. The true distance to A is zero.

38
00:04:37,500 --> 00:04:45,000
Dijkstra settles A at one, before B is even considered, and never revisits it. It reports two for T where the answer is one.

39
00:04:45,000 --> 00:04:52,500
Bellman-Ford relaxes every edge once per round, for one round fewer than there are vertices, and gets the true distances.

40
00:04:52,500 --> 00:05:00,000
Three more tools: one topological pass on a DAG, Floyd-Warshall for all pairs, and the rule that picks between them.

41
00:05:00,000 --> 00:05:07,500
Ask four questions in order. Is there a source, or do I need all pairs? Are there weights? Are any weights negative? Is the graph acyclic?

42
00:05:07,500 --> 00:05:15,000
BFS and DFS cost V plus E on a list and V squared on a matrix. Kruskal costs E log E. Prim costs E log V with a heap or V squared with an array.

43
00:05:15,000 --> 00:05:22,500
Dijkstra costs E log V, Bellman-Ford V times E, a DAG pass V plus E, and Floyd-Warshall V cubed.

44
00:05:22,500 --> 00:05:30,000
One sentence to keep: a minimum spanning tree minimises the total, a shortest-path tree minimises each route, and the two are different trees.

